<mujoco model="fr5 sdl cell">
  <!-- The FR5 + AG-95 in the SDL lab cell, for testing the perception path the
       physical rig will use: rendered camera -> apriltag_ros -> cho_object_pose
       -> a robot-frame pose per vessel -> the planner's World State.

       Selected with mujoco_scene:=scene_ag95_sdl.xml on the bringup; the plain
       scene_ag95.xml stays the default, so nothing that does not ask for the
       cell pays for it.

       The layout mirrors the Isaac cell one-for-one
       (isaacsim/scripts/standalone/task.py in sdl_project), because the point
       of this scene is to exercise the same geometry through a different
       renderer - a vessel somewhere else here would test a cell that does not
       exist anywhere:

         table top   z = 0, 1.5 m square, robot base at the origin
         beaker      (0.49,     0.17475, 0.0675)   0.05 x 0.05 x 0.135
         flask       (0.48383,  0.33166, 0.06)     0.07 x 0.07 x 0.12
         tag plate   vessel + (0.15, 0, *), lying on the table

       The vessels are drawn as cylinders because that is their shape; the
       planner models both as boxes of the footprints above, which is what
       cuTAMP's ENTITIES table says and is unchanged by anything here.

       THE OBJECTS ARE STATIC. Adding free joints would change nq and invalidate
       the `home`/`home1` keyframes the bringup selects by name, and the FR5
       model disables collisions outright (every geom is contype/conaffinity 0,
       see fr5_ag95.xml) so nothing could be grasped here anyway. This scene
       tests perception -> World State -> planning -> trajectory execution. It
       does not test grasping. -->

  <include file="fr5_ag95.xml"/>
  <include file="scene_common.xml"/>

  <asset>
    <!-- Rendered by TAMP/tamp/scripts/perception/generate_tag_images.py in sdl_project,
         which verifies each image decodes back to the id it encodes before
         writing it. 10 cells across, of which the BLACK SQUARE is 8, so a
         125 mm plate carries a 100 mm tag.

         100 mm and not the 40 mm the wrist-camera task uses, because this
         camera is 1.95 m away: at fx = 617 px a 40 mm tag spans 12.6 px, and
         cho_object_pose rejects anything under a 25 px shortest edge as too
         far or too oblique. Measured: it rejected both tags at exactly 12.3 and
         12.7 px. 100 mm gives 31.6 px, which clears the gate with margin. The
         same arithmetic applies to the physical cell at that standoff. -->
    <texture type="2d" name="tag36h11_00" file="assets/tags/tag36h11_00.png"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="tag36h11_01" file="assets/tags/tag36h11_01.png"/>
    <!-- No specular and no reflectance: a highlight across a tag face is the
         one lighting artefact that costs the quad detector a corner. -->
    <material name="tag36h11_00" texture="tag36h11_00" specular="0" shininess="0" reflectance="0"/>
    <material name="tag36h11_01" texture="tag36h11_01" specular="0" shininess="0" reflectance="0"/>
    <material name="bench" rgba="0.76 0.76 0.78 1" specular="0.1" shininess="0.1" reflectance="0"/>
    <material name="glassware" rgba="0.82 0.88 0.92 1" specular="0.3" shininess="0.3" reflectance="0"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- Table. Top face at z = 0, which is where the vessel heights below are
         measured from, and 1.5 m square to match the 0.70 m workspace bound the
         randomizer keeps objects inside. -->
    <geom name="bench" type="box" pos="0 0 -0.01" size="0.75 0.75 0.01" material="bench"/>

    <!-- Vessels. Half-extents: radius = footprint / 2, half-height = height / 2. -->
    <geom name="beaker" type="cylinder" pos="0.49 0.17475 0.0675" size="0.025 0.0675"
          material="glassware"/>
    <geom name="flask" type="cylinder" pos="0.48383 0.33166 0.06" size="0.035 0.06"
          material="glassware"/>

    <!-- Tag plates, 125 x 125 x 2 mm, lying flat 0.15 m along +x from their
         vessel - the same offset the Isaac vessels carry their tag at. Beside
         the vessel rather than on it so the vessel cannot occlude its own tag:
         at the camera's 50 deg elevation a 0.135 m beaker casts 0.113 m of
         shadow, which is shorter than the offset.

         Tag top face at z = 0.002 (plate half-thickness 0.001 on the table
         top). TAMP/tamp/scripts/perception/check_object_pose.py compares the pose
         cho_object_pose publishes against exactly these numbers. -->
    <geom name="beaker_tag" type="box" pos="0.64 0.17475 0.001" size="0.0625 0.0625 0.001"
          material="tag36h11_00"/>
    <geom name="flask_tag" type="box" pos="0.63383 0.33166 0.001" size="0.0625 0.0625 0.001"
          material="tag36h11_01"/>

    <!-- RealSense D435 colour stream, emulated. mujoco_ros2_control registers
         every camera in the model and derives CameraInfo from fovy and
         resolution as a distortion-free pinhole: at 640x480 with fovy 42.5 deg
         (the D435's colour vertical FOV) that gives fx = fy = 617.1 px, against
         the 617.2 px the real D435 reports at that resolution. So the detector
         sees the same scale here as it will on the bench, and no rectify hop is
         needed because the rendered image carries no distortion to remove.

         Pose and aim are the Isaac cell's camera_1: (1.45, 0, 1.55) looking at
         (0.20, 0, 0.05), i.e. 50.2 deg of elevation. quat is the look-at
         rotation for MuJoCo's camera convention (-z forward, +y up) and is
         read straight out of this file by the static TF publisher
         (TAMP/tamp/scripts/perception/mujoco_camera_tf.py), which converts it to the
         optical frame the detector reports in - so the two cannot drift. -->
    <camera name="sdl_cam" pos="1.45 0 1.55"
            quat="0.664872409 0.240717012 0.240717012 0.664872409"
            fovy="42.5" resolution="640 480"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
